								<div class="title" id="post-title-2369329">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2203795&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608954412" alt="Kalinin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2203795)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;" >Kalinin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1441301450 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">03 Sep 2015 17:30</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2369329">
									

<p>While this is an improvement over the original, which was awful, I don't think a fresh coat of paint and attaching Herman Fuller to the article really do much for the original concept.</p>
<p>I get that rewrites of stuff like this are intended these days to try to hew as close as possible to the spirit of the original article<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-303007-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-303007-1')">1</a></sup>, but there's nothing but cliches at the heart of this article. If these cliches were employed in the service of something, that might be okay, but they're just there for the sake of existing. Overpowered thing what kills you. And it makes you interact with it because we can up the body count that way.</p>
<p>The Herman Fuller connection doesn't feel right, either<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-303007-2" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-303007-2')">2</a></sup>. I had never really understood the primary purpose of the Circus of the Disquieting to be &quot;hurting people.&quot; And yet that's what this is, just a means of hurting people in a kind-of ironic way. It feels more at home as something The Joker would leave around in a Batman comic than it does on the mainlist, Series I or no.</p>
<p>To quote a great man in regards to the original concept, sometimes dead is better.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
